{
 "id": "2018-jiang-alley",
 "name": "Alley Interventions and Perceived Safety in Hong Kong",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli (one of its five sites): participants see the paper's 16 panoramic photo-simulations of one back-alley entrance in Yau Tsim Mong, Hong Kong (Figure 4, CC BY 4.0) in random order and rate each for perceived safety from 1 (very unsafe) to 5 (very safe). The scenes are the alley as it is, the cleaned alley, four vegetation designs (naturalistic or geometric green wall, with or without ground planting) and ten urban-function designs with greenery (art gallery, shop, cycle parking, gym, platform, café, pocket park). Analysis as in the paper: mean safety per scene and per category (baseline, cleaning, vegetation, urban function), comparisons with the cleaned scene and between geometric and naturalistic styles. Original study: 218 adults in Hong Kong; category means 2.42, 2.90, 3.29 and 3.75, so changing what an alley is used for raised perceived safety more than cleaning or planting alone.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) only the one site whose 16 scenes the paper prints is included; the original used five alleys (80 photos) and showed each person 30 of them at random, here everyone rates all 16; (2) the question wording \"How safe would you feel in this place?\" and the intro are mine (the paper gives only the very unsafe to very safe scale); (3) a residence question is added, and age is asked as a number instead of five-year bands.",
 "author": "Jiang, Mak, Zhong, Larsen & Webster",
 "year": "2018",
